REMOVE-SYNTHETIC-ACCOUNTS

The report run (37133260216) answered the question the previous refusal raised. Of the
eleven accounts in production, ten are at @example.invalid — the domain RFC 2606
reserves so that it can never belong to a person — and one is test@medtracker.com.
None is the address the owner gave, so they never registered on the rebuilt production
with it.

Ten of the eleven therefore need no decision from anybody: an address at .invalid
cannot be a real person's, whichever tool registered it. This removes those, and leaves
test@medtracker.com for the owner to confirm.

The report also found one medication and one package created after the purge, so
somebody is already using the site. If that data belongs to a .invalid household the
foreign keys will refuse this cleanup and name the table, which is the protection
working rather than a fault.

Remove this file once the cleanup has run.
